cartridge "MICRO CITY/DARRYL DION HAWKINS/1986"

' MICRO CITY
' A ColecoVision game based on "Micro City" by Darryl Dion Hawkins, Ahoy!
' December 1986 (Commodore 64 BASIC; the author's name is on its title
' screen, "WRITTEN BY DARRYL DION HAWKINS"; orig/ has the program and its
' detokenized listing). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a street fighter cleaning up Micro City, one thug at a time.
' Eight rounds on a city sidewalk at night; knock the thug down before he
' knocks you down. Win all eight and the city is saved; lose once and the
' game is over.
'
' Kept from the original (rules read from the listing, lines 1000-3690):
' eight rounds; the thug's skill, step and health by round, (1, 8, 25),
' (1, 12, 30), (1, 12, 35), (2, 8, 25), (2, 12, 25), (3, 8, 20),
' (3, 12, 25), (3, 12, 30) (the listing's ON SC list, which skips its
' line 3660); you start each round with 20 health; up is a high punch (2
' damage), down a low punch (3 damage, slower); a low punch is blocked by
' a fighter standing still (not walking, not ducking, not punching); the
' button ducks and a high punch passes over; the button with down rests
' (+1 health, a rest dodges both punches, eight rests a round); the game
' starts at round 1, 3 or 6; a score and a high score.
'
' Liberties (the port is a real-time brawler; the original ran in turns,
' you then the thug, about five a second):
' - Everything runs every frame: the stick is read each frame, you walk
'   1.25 pixels a frame, the thug 1 (step 12) or 0.75 (step 8) a frame.
' - Punches have a wind-up, three active frames and a recovery: high 6 +
'   3 + 8 frames, low 10 + 3 + 14 for you; the thug winds up longer (high
'   12, 10 or 8 frames by skill, low 6 more). A punch is decided on its
'   first active frame (in reach: 27 pixels high, 24 low). A hit stuns
'   (14 frames high, 20 low), knocks back (4 or 7 pixels), freezes the
'   action for 4 frames with a spark and a shake; a block pushes 2 pixels.
'   A hit interrupts the other fighter's punch, duck or walk.
' - A rest lasts 40 frames; the health comes at its end (up to 28).
' - Knocked down means the round is over (the original's health 0).
' - The thug, every frame, by his skill: he walks in until he is in reach
'   (he never stops out of reach), takes a stance (ducking or standing
'   guard, ducking more against a player who punches high), sees your
'   punch after a reaction delay (17-22 frames for skill 1, 12-17 for 2,
'   7-12 for 3) and ducks a high one or stands up to block a low one; he
'   learns which punch follows which (four counters) and, after three or
'   four alike, reacts at once; he hears a steady beat (punches at the
'   same interval) and gets ready just before the next is due; after
'   dodging or blocking he counters (low if your punch has long to run,
'   else high); he punches when you are open (not winding up), mixing high
'   and low (low at a duck from skill 2), waits 24/18/12 frames or more
'   between punches, and backs off now and then. Holding one direction or
'   button, or a fixed rhythm, loses.
' - Positions in quarter pixels; the fighters 16 to 22 pixels apart at the
'   closest; poses for walking (three frames), wind-up and blow of each
'   punch, duck, rest, hit, falling and down.
' - Score: 20 a high punch that lands, 30 a low punch, 100 x the round +
'   10 x your health for each round won. Health shown as bars.
'
' Controls: either controller. Left and right walk, up punches high, down
' punches low, a button ducks, a button with down rests. On the title,
' left and right choose the starting round, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Nothing flashes.

